            {
              "id": "glog-7a573cf4-fd07-47a2-a1ee-1eb36cb529e4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behaviors.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the `strlen(env_prefix)` function is a potential vulnerability sink if `env_prefix` is not a null-terminated string.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your strings.\n\nAlso, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes an additional parameter to specify the maximum number of characters to be examined. This can prevent buffer overflows by limiting the number of characters `strnlen()` reads.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nIf `env_prefix` is a character array, ensure it is null-terminated:\n\n```cpp\nchar env_prefix[] = \"prefix\";\n```\n\nIf `env_prefix` is a pointer to a character, ensure the string it points to is null-terminated:\n\n```cpp\nchar* env_prefix = new char[7];\nstrcpy(env_prefix, \"prefix\");\n```\n\nOr use `strnlen()` instead of `strlen()`:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = strnlen(env_prefix, MAX_LEN);\n```\n\nWhere `MAX_LEN` is the maximum expected length of `env_prefix`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C Standard Library, so you need to include the `cstring` or `string.h` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
